🏗️ Architecture & Design
4. Module-level logging.basicConfig()
Calling basicConfig at import time configures the root logger. If this module is imported into a larger application that has its own logging setup, this can silently override it.
Fix: Remove basicConfig from the module and let the application entry point configure logging. Your per-module getLogger(name) is already correct.
5. SessionState is both data and service
Using a @dataclass for something that owns threads, locks, and background timers is legal Python, but it blurs the line between a plain data container and a long-lived service object. This can surprise consumers who expect dataclasses to be cheaply copyable/serializable. It works, but be aware that pickling, deep-copying, or dataclass introspection tools may trip over the non-persisted threading fields.
6. from_dict() acquires a lock on a brand-new instance
state = cls()
with state._lock:  # harmless, but unnecessary
No other thread can possibly hold a reference to state yet, so the lock does nothing here.
🛡️ Robustness
7. load_from_file() lacks error handling
If the file is missing, corrupted, or contains malformed JSON, this will propagate a raw exception to the caller.
Fix: Wrap in a try/except and return a fresh SessionState() on failure, or raise a domain-specific exception.
8. No validation on interval_sec
Passing 0 or a negative number to start_periodic_save will create a busy-loop.
9. Data loss risk on abrupt exit
The saver thread is a daemon, so the process will exit without waiting for it. If the process crashes or receives SIGTERM, the last up-to-60-seconds of state may be lost. Consider an atexit hook or explicit stop_periodic_save() calls in your shutdown path.
✨ Minor Polish
•  asdict is imported but unused — you can drop it.
•  RingBuffer.push always returns True — the return value serves no purpose.
•  MAX_SENSORY_SAMPLES / MAX_EPISODIC_LOG are module-level constants — consider allowing them to be constructor arguments for different deployment profiles.
•  all would make the public API explicit.
Bottom Line
The persistence layer (save_to_file / load_from_file) and the dataclass design are solid. The biggest issue is the broken RingBuffer (remove it or fix it), followed by the threading race in start_periodic_save. Fix those two, guard load_from_file, and move basicConfig out of the module, and this becomes production-grade scaffolding for an episodic memory system.
